---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionTitle from "../components/SectionTitle.astro";
import OresCode from "../components/OresCode.astro";

const actorCode = `
define actor Worker as
  pub fnc on_start(): void {
    stdio.println("worker ready");
  }

  pub fnc run(Job job): Result {
    return process(job);
  }
end

const worker = spawn Worker();
const result = await worker.run(job);
`;

const selectCode = `
do nb select {
  when readch messages: val msg {
    stdio.println("got: ", msg);
  }

  when await future: val result {
    consume(result);
  }

  when timeout 5s {
    retry();
  }

  when cancelled token {
    cleanup();
  }
}
`;

const structCode = `
const account = infer struct {
  id: "acct_42",
  active: true
};

match account over
  on structural {id: string, active: bool} a -> {
    return a.id;
  }
  on _ -> {
    return "unknown";
  }
end
`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Oreslang — isolation-first systems programming"
  description="Oreslang is an isolation-first language for actors, FaaS, mobile runtimes, memory safety, hot code loading, and explicit compilation."
  home={true}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Isolation-first language design</p>
      <h1><span class="display-word">oreslang</span><br />safe code moves fast.</h1>
      <p class="hero-copy">
        A language for actor-based systems where isolation, option-safe data, hot code loading,
        explicit compilation, and predictable concurrency are part of the model—not a pile of conventions added later.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-note">
      <strong>Built for hostile boundaries.</strong>
      <p>Mobile sandboxes. FaaS runtimes. Multi-tenant actors. Trusted and untrusted code living in one system without sharing the wrong things.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="01 / About" title="Why Oreslang" href="/about/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Oreslang starts from a different default: code units should have explicit boundaries,
          actor messages should be safe to move, and the compiler should reject whole categories
          of lifetime and data-flow mistakes before the runtime needs to clean them up.
        </p>
        <div class="card-grid">
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Isolation first</h3>
            <p>Actors are not just syntax for queues. Shared, private, isolated, and untrusted actors carry different safety and visibility contracts.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Compile-time leverage</h3>
            <p>Borrowing, structural types, options, sendability rules, and AOT-friendly shapes make data movement visible to the compiler.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Code sharing without data leaks</h3>
            <p>Actors in one OresVM can share immutable code images while retaining isolated state and controlled capabilities.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Runtime plurality</h3>
            <p>The model is meant to fit phones, server processes, FaaS workers, GPU paths, and other constrained execution environments.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          Oreslang is authored by <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware">Oresoftware Dev House</a>.
          Click the section title to open the dedicated About page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-philosophy" id="philosophy">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="02 / Philosophy" title="Spend syntax to buy certainty" href="/philosophy/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="manifesto-lede">
          In the age of AI-assisted programming, terseness is less sacred. Oreslang is more explicit
          when those extra tokens reduce ambiguity and make static analysis, AOT compilation, and
          JIT specialization faster.
        </p>

        <div class="terminal-trace terminal-trace-home" aria-label="Illustrative Oreslang runtime stack trace">
          <div class="terminal-bar">
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <strong>ores · runtime</strong>
          </div>
          <pre><code><span class="term-prompt">$</span> ores run app.ores --mode=hybrid
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> loaded code image app.aot <span class="term-tag">AOT</span>
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> Worker.run crossed hot threshold <span class="term-tag">JIT tier 2</span>

<span class="term-error">OresError[E_OPTION_NONE]</span>: unwrap() called on Option.none
  --> src/worker.ores:18:14
  18 | const job = next.unwrap();
     |                  ^^^^^^

stack trace:
  0: Worker.run(Job)          src/worker.ores:18
  1: QueueActor.dispatch(Job) src/queue.ores:44
  2: actor#worker-17.mailbox  &lt;oresvm&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="compile-strip">
          <strong>AOT for startup. JIT for heat.</strong>
          <p>
            Oreslang's explicit static surface is designed to feed both ahead-of-time compilation for
            predictable startup and deployment, and runtime specialization of hot code paths when a JIT is available.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="keyword-rail" aria-label="Oreslang design themes">
          <span>borrow checker</span>
          <span>no nulls</span>
          <span>static types</span>
          <span>exhaustive match</span>
          <span>hybrid GC</span>
          <span>hot reload</span>
          <span>non-cooperative scheduling</span>
          <span>GPU / quantum</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The safety stack is layered: Rust-class memory-safety goals through ownership and borrowing,
          option types instead of null, automatic GC for managed graphs, actor confinement for easier
          concurrency safety, and explicit effect/error boundaries such as <code>pure</code> and <code>trap</code>.
          <code>alex</code>/<code>nlex</code> lexical modes are designed to expose more optimization intent to the compiler.
        </p>

        <div class="future-strip">
          <strong>Future: location-transparent actors.</strong>
          <p>
            Keep one typed actor model while placement evolves from local shared memory to isolated memory,
            IPC, TCP, or an external machine. Placement affinity should change transport—not application semantics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p><a href="/philosophy/">Read the full philosophy, runtime direction, and language-building notes →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-paradigms" id="paradigms">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="03 / Paradigms" title="More than one way to be fast" href="/paradigms/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="manifesto-lede">
          Oreslang is intentionally multi-paradigm: actor isolation for concurrency, functional
          composition for data flow, data-oriented layouts for throughput, hot-loading for change,
          and contracts for large static codebases.
        </p>

        <div class="paradigm-grid">
          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>ACTORS</span>
            <h3>Actor model + Graal isolates</h3>
            <p>Use actors as the primary concurrency boundary, with isolated execution available when mutable memory or third-party code must be confined.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>FUNCTIONAL</span>
            <h3>Functions as values</h3>
            <p>First-class functions, native map/filter/reduce, rx-ores style composition, and tail-call optimization for recursive functional workloads.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>DATA</span>
            <h3>Data-oriented programming</h3>
            <p>Prefer explicit shapes, compact data, predictable access patterns, and transformations that make memory movement visible to the compiler.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>HOT LOAD</span>
            <h3>Development and production</h3>
            <p>Reload code during development, or introduce a new isolated actor generation in production and move traffic without mutating the old actor in place.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>COMPILE</span>
            <h3>AOT, JIT, or hybrid</h3>
            <p>AOT for predictable startup, JIT for hot-path specialization, or a hybrid model that starts from compiled code and optimizes what becomes hot.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>CONTRACTS</span>
            <h3>Static architecture</h3>
            <p>Module contracts and static-member contracts for classes let APIs and static surfaces be checked without pretending modules and classes are the same thing.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p><a href="/paradigms/">Explore Oreslang's programming and runtime paradigms →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="examples">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="04 / Examples" title="The language in motion" href="/examples/" />
      <div class="code-stack">
        <OresCode caption="Actors with explicit lifecycle" code={actorCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Select over channels, futures, timeouts, and cancellation" code={selectCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Closed inferred structs + structural matching" code={structCode} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="problem-spaces">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="05 / Problem spaces" title="Where the sharp edges are" href="/problem-spaces/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The language is aimed at systems where ordinary process boundaries are too expensive,
          ordinary shared memory is too risky, dynamic code loading needs a stronger trust model,
          and workloads cannot always be trusted to cooperate with a scheduler.
        </p>
        <div class="problem-list">
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Isolation on phones and FaaS</h3>
            <p>Use actors and capabilities to create cheap boundaries inside one process, with stronger isolation for workloads that need it.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>True data safety</h3>
            <p>Option-aware APIs, compiler-enforced ownership/borrowing, immutable values, and serialized actor mailboxes reduce ambiguous lifetimes and pointer leaks.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Hot-loading trusted and untrusted code</h3>
            <p>Load code without duplicating immutable code images across every actor, while whitelisting what untrusted actors are allowed to execute or share.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Scheduling code that will not yield</h3>
            <p>FaaS and mobile runtimes need a path for CPU-hungry or third-party work that cannot rely on cooperative scheduling alone.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="links">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="06 / Links" title="Follow the source" href="/links/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The project is being designed and implemented in the open.</p>
        <div class="link-list">
          <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang"><span>Oreslang GitHub organization</span><span>↗</span></a>
          <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang/oreslang-source.java"><span>Compiler / VM source</span><span>↗</span></a>
          <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware"><span>Oresoftware Dev House</span><span>↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
